meta {
  name: Provision person in CAS [outcome unverified]
  type: http
  seq: 14
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/persons/provision
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "username": "kavya.nair",
    "email": "kavya.nair@example.com",
    "phone": "+91-9800000002",
    "first_name": "Kavya",
    "last_name": "Nair",
    "display_name": "Kavya Nair",
    "group_keys": ["teachers"]
  }
}

tests {
  // There is no status assert here on purpose. Nobody on this team has run
  // this request against a live CAS, and an assert would read as a statement
  // of fact about behaviour we have only reasoned about. This test accepts any
  // outcome we can explain, names which one happened, and fails on anything
  // else - including 401, which means you ran without a token and learnt
  // nothing about provisioning.
  //
  // Once you have seen the answer, replace this block with a plain assert.
  const explained = [201, 501, 502];
  const meaning = {
    201: "CAS created the identity. Provisioning works: assert 201, and read the note in request 18 about what folder 12 then has to delete.",
    501: "this service refused before calling CAS - no caller token on the request, or CAS offers this client no user service at all.",
    502: "the call reached CAS and CAS answered badly. Look at the server log for the status CAS returned.",
    401: "you ran without a token, so this request says nothing about provisioning either way."
  };

  test("provisioning answers something we can account for", function () {
    const status = res.getStatus();
    console.log("POST /persons/provision returned " + status + " - " +
      (meaning[status] || "an outcome none of us predicted; work out why before changing this test"));
    expect(explained).to.include(status);
  });
}

docs {
  UNVERIFIED. Read the tests block before you trust anything here.

  What this route is for: one call that mints the CAS identity and writes the
  emerge-core person row against it, instead of the two-step dance requests 01
  to 07 do (create the user in CAS by hand, then POST /persons with their
  cas_user_id). Omit password and CAS generates one, returned once in the
  response body and never stored here.

  What it answers is an open question, because it is a property of CAS's
  behaviour rather than of our code, and we have not run it:

  201 - the likely one. internal/infrastructure/cas/admin.go posts to the REST
  endpoint /v1/admin/users, and that call was extended once before to carry
  group_id so that CAS writes the user and their group membership in one
  transaction. That work was done against production CAS and it worked, which
  is decent evidence the endpoint is there and answers.

  502 - if CAS answers non-2xx for any reason other than 401, 403 or 409, the
  error falls through to the default arm of writeProvisioningErr and is
  reported as a bad gateway: CAS spoke, and we could not use what it said.

  501 - only in three narrow cases: the caller sent no token to forward (CAS
  admin routes read a session from it, so a service token will not do), CAS is
  not configured at all, or the lookup half of the flow is unavailable.

  An older internal note said 501 was the settled answer "until a
  cas.v1.UserService lands". That sentence is about a gRPC service and this
  code path is REST, so treat the note as stale rather than as an answer.
}
